1. IN Operator 
WHERE city IN ('Delhi', 'Mumbai', 'Pune') is equivalent to WHERE city = 'Delhi' OR city = 'Mumbai' OR city = 'Pune'
Cleaner, more readable, and usually faster than chaining OR conditions
The list MUST be enclosed in parentheses: IN (...)
String values need single quotes: IN ('Delhi', 'Mumbai')
Numeric values do NOT need quotes: IN (45000, 60000)
No trailing comma after the last item
Empty list IN () is a syntax error
Single-value list IN ('Delhi') works but is pointless — just use =
NOT IN negates the check: returns rows where the value is NOT in the list

2. ANY / ALL / SOME with Arrays 
= ANY(ARRAY[...]) is equivalent to IN (...)
> ALL(ARRAY[40000, 50000]) means "greater than EVERY value" (simplifies to > max)
> ANY(ARRAY[40000, 50000]) means "greater than AT LEAST ONE value" (simplifies to > min)
< ALL(ARRAY[50000, 60000]) means "less than EVERY value" (simplifies to < min)
= ALL(ARRAY[40000, 50000]) is impossible (can't equal two different values) → zero rows
SOME is a complete synonym for ANY — identical behavior
ANY/ALL with ARRAY[...] syntax is PostgreSQL-specific (native array support)
ANY/ALL with subqueries is universal SQL
For array COLUMNS (like tags TEXT[]), you MUST use = ANY(tags) — IN cannot compare against array columns directly

3. NOT IN + NULL Bug 
WHERE id NOT IN (1, 2, NULL) returns ZERO rows — always, silently, no error
Why: Comparing any value to NULL yields UNKNOWN. NOT (FALSE OR FALSE OR UNKNOWN) = NOT UNKNOWN = UNKNOWN. UNKNOWN is not TRUE, so the row is excluded. This happens for EVERY row.
Three-valued logic: SQL has TRUE, FALSE, and UNKNOWN. Only TRUE rows pass WHERE.
The fix — Option 1: Filter NULLs out: WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT col FROM t WHERE col IS NOT NULL)
The fix — Option 2: Use NOT EXISTS (full syntax Day 37): checks row existence, not value equality. NULL doesn't poison existence checks.
This bug is UNIVERSAL — affects PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server, Oracle, SQLite equally.

4. Safety Habits 
ALWAYS run your WHERE clause as a SELECT first before switching to UPDATE or DELETE
Verify the exact rows that will be affected before executing destructive operations
Transactions (preview, full coverage Day 55-58): BEGIN; ... COMMIT; or ROLLBACK; provides a safety net for manual data fixes
